In .NET MAUI, the `Application` class no longer has `OnStart`, `OnSleep`, and `OnResume` virtual methods. Instead, use the **lifecycle events** on the `Window` object:

```csharp
public partial class App : Application
{
    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
    {
        var window = new Window(new MainPage());

        window.Created += (s, e) => { /* replaces OnStart */ };
        window.Stopped += (s, e) => { /* replaces OnSleep */ };
        window.Resumed += (s, e) => { /* replaces OnResume */ };

        return window;
    }
}
```

**Key mappings:**
| Xamarin.Forms | .NET MAUI (`Window` event) |
|---------------|---------------------------|
| `OnStart()`   | `Window.Created`           |
| `OnSleep()`   | `Window.Stopped`           |
| `OnResume()`  | `Window.Resumed`           |

MAUI also exposes additional window events like `Activated`, `Deactivated`, `Backgrounding`, and `Destroying` for finer-grained control. For platform-specific lifecycle hooks, you can use `ConfigureLifecycleEvents` in `MauiProgram.cs`.
